    "reasons": "The formalization does not faithfully capture the original problem. Most importantly, it never states that O is the circumcenter of triangle ABC, e.g. OA = OB = OC or O being the center of the circumcircle. Since E is defined using line AO, omitting the circumcenter condition allows O to be essentially arbitrary, so the configuration and the claimed value of BX are not the same mathematical problem. The acute-triangle hypothesis is also not encoded correctly: h₀ only says an angle condition for points P in the interior relative to AB, rather than asserting that the triangle is acute at all three vertices, although this is partly redundant with the fixed side lengths. The altitude condition for D is ambiguous/incorrect as written: instead of stating that line AD is perpendicular to line BC, it uses `D ∈ perpendicular ℝ {A} (line[ℝ, B, C])`; depending on the actual meaning of `perpendicular`, this does not clearly express AD ⟂ BC and may not typecheck as intended. Additionally, h₇ introduces a new existential Y that shadows the outer Y parameter, leaving the outer Y unused, and the betweenness condition for X uses non-strict segment membership. Thus the theorem statement is structurally not a correct formalization of the intended geometry problem.",
